## Implementation Guidelines

1. **Start with theme structure** - Ensure `layout/theme.liquid` has proper landmarks
2. **Focus on key user journeys** - Homepage, product pages, cart, checkout
3. **Test early and often** - Use automated tools during development
4. **Include real users** - Test with people who use assistive technologies
5. **Document accessibility features** - Maintain clear documentation for ongoing compliance

## Legal Compliance Notes

- Shopify stores fall under ADA Title III as "places of public accommodation"
- WCAG 2.1 AA is the accepted standard for legal compliance
- Regular accessibility audits are recommended
- Consider obtaining accessibility certification for legal protection
- Keep detailed records of accessibility improvements and testing

## Advanced Accessibility Patterns

### Navigation Menu Accessibility
Implement accessible dropdown and mega menus to ensure all users can navigate your store effectively.

**Accessibility Impact:** Screen reader users need proper announcements when menus open/close. Keyboard users require logical tab order and escape mechanisms. Mobile users need touch-friendly interactions.

**Key Requirements:**
- Menu buttons must announce their expanded/collapsed state
- Submenus should be hidden from screen readers when closed
- Keyboard navigation with arrow keys, Enter, and Escape
- Focus management when opening/closing menus
- Proper ARIA labeling for menu relationships

**Common Issues Solved:**
- Menu items announced even when visually hidden
- Keyboard users getting trapped in dropdown menus
- Screen readers not understanding menu hierarchy
- Mobile users unable to access dropdown content

**Implementation Considerations:**
- Use `aria-expanded` to indicate menu state changes
- Set `tabindex="-1"` on hidden menu items to prevent keyboard access
- Implement proper focus trapping within mega menus
- Provide clear visual and programmatic focus indicators
- Ensure touch targets meet 44px minimum size requirement

**Demo Pattern:**
```
Line 45: <nav role="navigation" aria-label="Main navigation">
Line 46: <button aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="submenu-1">Categories</button>
Line 47: <ul id="submenu-1" aria-hidden="true">
Line 48: <li><a href="/collections/..." tabindex="-1">Collection Name</a></li>
```

**Check in:** `snippets/component-nav-dropdown.liquid`, `snippets/component-nav-megamenu.liquid`

### Search Functionality
Make search accessible with proper announcements and keyboard navigation to ensure all customers can find products effectively.

**Accessibility Impact:** Search is critical for e-commerce success. Screen reader users need clear instructions and result announcements. Keyboard users require proper focus management through search suggestions. Users with cognitive disabilities benefit from clear search feedback and error handling.

**Key Requirements:**
- Search form must be identified with `role="search"`
- Input field needs descriptive labeling and instructions
- Search results must be announced to screen readers
- Predictive/autocomplete results need keyboard navigation
- Search errors and empty states require clear communication
- Search suggestions should be properly labeled and selectable

**Common Issues Solved:**
- Screen readers not identifying search functionality
- Search results appearing without announcement
- Keyboard users unable to navigate search suggestions
- Unclear search instructions leading to user confusion
- Search errors not being communicated accessibly

**Implementation Considerations:**
- Use `aria-live="polite"` for search result announcements
- Implement proper keyboard navigation (Arrow keys, Enter, Escape)
- Provide search instructions that screen readers can access
- Handle empty search states with helpful messaging
- Ensure search suggestions have proper ARIA labeling
- Consider search history and saved searches for returning users

**Demo Pattern:**
```
Line 62: <form role="search" aria-label="Site search">
Line 63: <input type="search" aria-describedby="search-instructions" autocomplete="off">
Line 64: <div id="search-instructions" class="sr-only">Type to search products</div>
Line 65: <div role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
Line 66: Found {{ search.results_count }} results for "{{ search.terms }}"
```

**Check in:** `snippets/component-predictive-search.liquid`, `sections/main-search.liquid`

### Collection Filters and Sorting
Ensure filter controls are accessible and announce changes to help all users refine product searches effectively.

**Accessibility Impact:** Filters are essential for product discovery in e-commerce. Screen reader users need clear filter labels and result announcements. Users with motor disabilities require accessible range sliders and checkbox controls. Cognitive accessibility benefits from clear filter states and easy reset options.

**Key Requirements:**
- Filter groups must use `fieldset` and `legend` elements for proper grouping
- Range sliders need `aria-valuemin`, `aria-valuemax`, and `aria-valuenow` attributes
- Filter changes should announce updated product counts
- Applied filters must be clearly indicated and easily removable
- Sort options need proper labeling and current selection indication
- Filter reset functionality should be keyboard accessible

**Common Issues Solved:**
- Screen readers not understanding filter relationships
- Range sliders without accessible value announcements
- Filter changes not communicated to assistive technologies
- Users unable to understand which filters are currently active
- Complex filter interfaces causing navigation confusion

**Implementation Considerations:**
- Group related filters using `fieldset` with descriptive `legend`
- Use `aria-live="polite"` for non-urgent filter result updates
- Provide visual and programmatic indication of active filters
- Implement clear "Clear all filters" functionality
- Consider filter persistence across page navigation
- Ensure mobile filter interfaces are touch-accessible

**Demo Pattern:**
```
Line 78: <fieldset><legend>Filter by Price</legend>
Line 79: <input type="range" aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="1000" aria-valuenow="250">
Line 80: <output for="price-range">$250</output>
Line 81: <div aria-live="polite">Showing {{ collection.products_count }} products</div>
```

**Check in:** `snippets/component-filters-*.liquid`, `sections/main-collection.liquid`

### Pagination Accessibility
Implement accessible page navigation with proper labeling to ensure users can navigate through product collections efficiently.

**Accessibility Impact:** Pagination is crucial for large product catalogs. Screen reader users need clear page navigation context. Keyboard users require logical tab order through page links. Users with cognitive disabilities benefit from clear current page indicators and navigation options.

**Key Requirements:**
- Pagination must be wrapped in `<nav>` with descriptive `aria-label`
- Current page should be indicated with `aria-current="page"`
- Previous/Next links need descriptive `aria-label` attributes
- Page numbers should be clearly distinguishable from navigation
- Skip to first/last page options for long pagination lists
- Clear indication of total pages and current position

**Common Issues Solved:**
- Screen readers not identifying pagination controls
- Unclear current page indication for assistive technologies
- Confusing navigation for users with cognitive disabilities
- Keyboard navigation difficulties through page numbers
- Lack of context about total pages or current position

**Implementation Considerations:**
- Use semantic `<nav>` element with proper labeling
- Provide context like "Page 2 of 15" for screen readers
- Implement keyboard shortcuts for power users (Ctrl+arrows)
- Consider infinite scroll with accessible load more buttons
- Ensure pagination works with browser back/forward buttons
- Provide jump-to-page functionality for long lists

**Demo Pattern:**
```
Line 92: <nav aria-label="Pagination navigation">
Line 93: <a href="..." aria-label="Go to previous page">← Previous</a>
Line 94: <a href="..." aria-current="page">2</a>
Line 95: <a href="..." aria-label="Go to next page">Next →</a>
```

**Check in:** `snippets/component-pagination.liquid`

## E-commerce Specific Accessibility

### Product Comparison
Make product comparison tables accessible to help customers make informed purchasing decisions regardless of their abilities.

**Accessibility Impact:** Product comparison is crucial for e-commerce conversion. Screen reader users need proper table structure to understand product relationships. Users with cognitive disabilities benefit from clear comparison layouts. Keyboard users require easy navigation through comparison data.

**Key Requirements:**
- Use semantic `<table>` elements with proper headers
- Provide descriptive `<caption>` explaining comparison purpose
- Use `<th scope="col">` for product names and `<th scope="row">` for features
- Include summary information for complex comparisons
- Ensure comparison controls are keyboard accessible
- Provide alternative text for comparison charts or graphs

**Common Issues Solved:**
- Screen readers unable to understand product relationships
- Complex comparison data without proper structure
- Visual-only comparison indicators without text alternatives
- Keyboard navigation difficulties through comparison tables
- Unclear comparison criteria for users with cognitive disabilities

**Implementation Considerations:**
- Use `<caption>` to describe what's being compared
- Implement proper header associations with `scope` attributes
- Consider providing comparison summaries for complex data
- Ensure comparison controls work with keyboard navigation
- Add "Compare" button states with proper ARIA attributes
- Provide easy removal of products from comparison

**Demo Pattern:**
```
Line 108: <table role="table" aria-label="Product comparison">
Line 109: <caption>Compare {{ products.size }} selected products</caption>
Line 110: <thead><tr><th scope="col">Feature</th><th scope="col">{{ product.title }}</th></tr></thead>
Line 111: <tbody><tr><th scope="row">Price</th><td>{{ product.price | money }}</td></tr></tbody>
```

**Check in:** `sections/product-comparison.liquid`

### Product Reviews and Ratings
Ensure reviews are accessible to screen readers to help all customers make informed decisions based on social proof.

**Accessibility Impact:** Reviews and ratings are key trust signals in e-commerce. Screen reader users need ratings communicated clearly as text, not just visual stars. Users with cognitive disabilities benefit from clear review structures and helpful/unhelpful voting systems.

**Key Requirements:**
- Star ratings must have text equivalents using `aria-label`
- Use `role="img"` for star rating displays with descriptive labels
- Review content should be properly structured with headings
- Voting buttons (helpful/unhelpful) need clear labeling
- Review pagination should follow accessibility guidelines
- Review forms must be fully accessible with proper error handling

**Common Issues Solved:**
- Star ratings invisible to screen readers
- Review content without proper structure or navigation
- Voting mechanisms without clear purpose indication
- Review filtering controls inaccessible to keyboard users
- Unclear reviewer information and verification status

**Implementation Considerations:**
- Always provide text alternatives for visual ratings
- Structure reviews with proper headings and landmarks
- Implement accessible review filtering and sorting
- Ensure review submission forms follow form accessibility guidelines
- Consider review helpfulness indicators for screen readers
- Provide clear reviewer verification and date information

**Demo Pattern:**
```
Line 125: <div role="img" aria-label="4.5 out of 5 stars">★★★★☆</div>
