## Loading States and Progressive Enhancement

### Skeleton Screens
Provide accessible loading states to reduce perceived loading time and maintain user engagement during content loading.

**Accessibility Impact:** Loading states prevent user confusion and anxiety during content loading. Screen reader users need to understand that content is loading. Users with slower connections benefit from clear loading indicators. Skeleton screens provide better UX than blank screens.

**Key Requirements:**
- Loading containers must use `role="status"` for screen reader announcements
- Skeleton elements should be hidden from screen readers with `aria-hidden="true"`
- Loading messages must be announced with screen reader friendly text
- Loading states should respect reduced motion preferences
- Provide estimated loading times when possible
- Ensure loading states don't interfere with existing content

**Common Issues Solved:**
- Users thinking the page is broken during loading
- Screen readers not announcing loading states
- Loading animations causing motion sensitivity issues
- Unclear progress indication for long operations
- Loading states blocking interaction with loaded content

**Implementation Considerations:**
- Use `aria-live="polite"` for non-urgent loading announcements
- Implement skeleton screens that match final content structure
- Provide loading cancellation options for long operations
- Consider offline states and connection quality
- Ensure loading states work without JavaScript
- Test loading states with various connection speeds

**Demo Pattern:**
```
Line 228: <div role="status" aria-label="Loading products">
Line 229: <div class="skeleton-product-card" aria-hidden="true"></div>
Line 230: <span class="sr-only">Loading product information, please wait...</span>
```

### Lazy Loading Images
Implement accessible image lazy loading to improve performance while maintaining accessibility for all users.

**Accessibility Impact:** Lazy loading improves page performance, especially important for users with limited bandwidth. Screen reader users need proper alt text even for lazy-loaded images. Loading indicators help users understand when images are being fetched.

**Key Requirements:**
- Lazy-loaded images must maintain proper alt text
- Loading placeholders should be accessible
- Image loading status should be communicated to screen readers
- Fallback behavior required when lazy loading fails
- Critical images should not be lazy loaded
- Loading indicators should be dismissible

**Common Issues Solved:**
- Images loading without accessibility context
- Missing alt text on lazy-loaded images
- Users not understanding when images are loading
- Poor performance on slower connections without proper feedback
- Lazy loading interfering with screen reader navigation

**Implementation Considerations:**
- Use `loading="lazy"` attribute with proper fallbacks
- Maintain alt text in data attributes during loading
- Provide meaningful loading placeholders
- Implement intersection observer with accessibility considerations
- Consider preloading critical above-the-fold images
- Test with various screen reader and browser combinations

**Demo Pattern:**
```
Line 244: <img loading="lazy" src="placeholder.jpg" data-src="product.jpg" 
Line 245: alt="{{ product.title }}" aria-describedby="loading-status">
Line 246: <div id="loading-status" class="sr-only" aria-live="polite">Image loading...</div>
```

### Progress Indicators
Show progress for long operations to keep users informed and reduce abandonment during extended processes.

**Accessibility Impact:** Progress indicators are crucial for users with disabilities who may need more time to complete tasks. Screen reader users need progress announcements. Visual progress indicators must have programmatic equivalents.

**Key Requirements:**
- Progress bars must use `role="progressbar"` with proper value attributes
- Progress updates should be announced to screen readers
- Provide both percentage and descriptive progress information
- Allow cancellation of long-running operations
- Include estimated completion times when possible
- Progress indicators should be keyboard focusable for status checking

**Common Issues Solved:**
- Users abandoning long operations without progress feedback
- Progress information only available visually
- Unclear progress status for screen reader users
- No way to cancel or pause long operations
- Progress indicators without meaningful completion estimates

**Implementation Considerations:**
- Use `aria-valuenow`, `aria-valuemin`, and `aria-valuemax` for progress bars
- Implement live region updates for progress announcements
- Provide both determinate and indeterminate progress indicators
- Consider progress persistence across page refreshes
- Include contextual information about what's being processed
- Test progress indicators with various assistive technologies

**Demo Pattern:**
```
Line 260: <div role="progressbar" aria-valuenow="65" aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="100" 
Line 261: aria-label="Upload progress">
Line 262: <div class="progress-bar" style="width: 65%"></div>
Line 263: <span class="sr-only">65% complete</span>
```

## Mobile and Touch Accessibility

### Touch Target Sizing
Ensure touch targets meet accessibility standards to support users with motor disabilities and improve mobile usability for all customers.

**Accessibility Impact:** Touch target sizing is critical for users with motor disabilities, tremors, or limited dexterity. Elderly users and users with large fingers benefit from appropriately sized touch targets. Mobile shopping conversion rates improve with accessible touch interfaces.

**Key Requirements:**
- All interactive elements must be at least 44x44 pixels (iOS) or 48x48 pixels (Android)
- Touch targets should have adequate spacing between them (minimum 8px)
- Nested interactive elements should be avoided
- Touch targets should provide visual and haptic feedback
- Consider different device orientations and screen sizes
- Ensure touch targets work with assistive touch technologies

**Common Issues Solved:**
- Small buttons difficult to tap on mobile devices
- Accidentally triggering adjacent interactive elements
- Touch targets too close together causing user frustration
- Inconsistent touch target sizes across the interface
- Interactive elements without proper touch feedback

**Implementation Considerations:**
- Use CSS to ensure minimum touch target sizes across all interactive elements
- Provide adequate spacing between touch targets
- Consider thumb reach and one-handed mobile usage patterns
- Test with various device sizes and orientations
- Implement proper touch feedback with CSS `:active` states
- Ensure touch targets scale appropriately with text size adjustments

**Demo Pattern:**
```
Line 277: button, .btn, a, input[type="submit"] { min-height: 44px; min-width: 44px; }
Line 278: .touch-target { padding: 12px; margin: 2px; } /* Ensure spacing */
```

### Swipe Gestures
Provide alternatives to swipe-only navigation to ensure all users can access carousel and slider content regardless of their interaction capabilities.

**Accessibility Impact:** Swipe-only navigation excludes users with motor disabilities who cannot perform swipe gestures. Screen reader users need alternative navigation methods. Switch control users require button-based navigation options.

**Key Requirements:**
- Always provide button alternatives to swipe gestures
- Swipe areas should be properly labeled for screen readers
- Keyboard navigation must be available for carousel content
- Auto-advancing carousels should be pausable
- Provide clear indication of current position and total items
- Support multiple navigation methods simultaneously

**Common Issues Solved:**
- Content accessible only through swipe gestures
- Carousels without keyboard navigation support
- Auto-advancing content causing motion sensitivity issues
- Unclear current position in carousel content
- Swipe gestures not working with assistive technologies

**Implementation Considerations:**
- Always include previous/next buttons alongside swipe functionality
- Use ARIA live regions to announce carousel changes
- Implement keyboard arrow key navigation for carousel items
- Provide pagination indicators with proper ARIA labeling
- Consider touch, mouse, and keyboard interaction patterns
- Test carousel functionality with various assistive technologies

